  "context_pill": "Capital Gains Exclusions",
  "example_summary": "Analyzing three specific scenarios—gift of gold, HUF partition distribution, and debenture conversion—to determine if they trigger capital gains tax under Section 47.",
  "source_provision": "### Statutory Summary Matrix: Exclusions from Transfer u/s 47\n\n| Statutory Section | Transaction Category | Statutory Condition & Scope | Tax Consequence & Treatment |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Section 47(iii)** | Gift or Will or Irrevocable Trust | Transfer of a capital asset by an individual or HUF under a gift, will, or irrevocable trust | Not regarded as transfer; **Zero capital gains tax liability** |\n| **Section 47(i)** | HUF Partition | Total or partial partition of a Hindu Undivided Family distributing capital assets in kind | Not regarded as transfer; **Zero capital gains tax liability** |\n| **Section 47(x)** | Conversion of Debentures | Conversion of bonds, debentures, debenture stock, or deposit certificates into shares or debentures of that company | Not regarded as transfer; **Zero capital gains tax liability** |\n\n\n> [!NOTE] Fundamental Prerequisite: Capital Gains Chargeability\n> As per **Section 45(1)**, any profits or gains arising from the transfer of a capital asset effected in the previous year shall be chargeable to income tax under the head Capital Gains. **Section 47** overrides this charging section by specifying transactions that do not constitute a transfer.\n\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] Subsequent Transfer Trap: Cost of Previous Owner\n> When an asset is acquired through a transaction covered under **Section 47(iii)** or **Section 47(i)**, the cost of acquisition for the subsequent holder will be determined under **Section 49(1)** as the cost to the previous owner, ensuring that the latent capital gains are preserved for taxation upon ultimate alienation to a third party.",
